WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.461 --> 00:00:03.264
- They sound a lot like voices

00:00:03.264 --> 00:00:05.301
or like an angry choir.

00:00:05.301 --> 00:00:06.965
Somebody told me that
they sound a lot like

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.027
sniffing a whole lot of
magic markers all at once.

00:00:10.027 --> 00:00:12.360
You can decide for yourself.

00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:19.758
I'm Andy Cavatorta, and I'm a sculptor.

00:00:19.758 --> 00:00:23.576
I work with robotics and with sound.

00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:25.993
(soft music)

00:00:29.784 --> 00:00:32.206
The work that I mostly
do involves creating

00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:33.944
new types of musical instruments

00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:36.174
or new types of physical sounds.

00:00:36.174 --> 00:00:37.614
A lot of times this has a lot to do

00:00:37.614 --> 00:00:39.364
with motion and form.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.800
What I'm sitting next to right here,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.165
it's called the Dervishes.

00:00:45.165 --> 00:00:47.597
It's got these 14 spinning arms.

00:00:47.597 --> 00:00:49.401
It's similar to the toys
that you played with

00:00:49.401 --> 00:00:51.843
as a kid where you might spin
a corrugated plastic tube

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:53.081
over your head.

00:00:53.081 --> 00:00:55.993
There's a lot of circuit boards
in here for motor control

00:00:55.993 --> 00:00:58.659
and we measure the speeds very precisely.

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:00.323
And then there's a lot of software

00:01:00.323 --> 00:01:03.545
that takes the specific pitches,

00:01:03.545 --> 00:01:05.401
figures out exactly where
they should be played

00:01:05.401 --> 00:01:07.566
and at what speeds.

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:10.670
It's a lot of fine tuning
all the way through

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.113
from the music that gets written for it

00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:15.619
to the software that
calibrates and adjusts

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:17.934
and controls it, all the way
through to the fine tuning

00:01:17.934 --> 00:01:20.101
and the motor controllers.

00:01:21.273 --> 00:01:23.225
What I do is a bit on a parallel track

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:27.171
with a lot of what's
happening in electronic music.

00:01:27.171 --> 00:01:29.859
But I work specifically,
almost exclusively,

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:32.803
with physical sound, almost
never with synthesized sound

00:01:32.803 --> 00:01:34.425
or samples or stuff like that.

00:01:34.425 --> 00:01:36.040
Just because those are the
ones that actually have

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.957
the most meaning to me.

00:01:39.243 --> 00:01:42.315
All my life I've been interested in sound.

00:01:42.315 --> 00:01:45.323
I think it's how I figure things out.

00:01:45.323 --> 00:01:48.715
I always love when
these instruments create

00:01:48.715 --> 00:01:52.882
the possibility to make music
that didn't exist before.

